new file mode 100644
index 0000000..5963e75
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/deployment.md
@@ -0,0 +1,146 @@
+# Deploying Fizzy on one box
+
+Fizzy is happy on a single Linux server: one container runs the web app and
+the background jobs, SQLite holds the data, uploads go on disk, and Kamal's
+proxy terminates SSL with an automatically renewed Let's Encrypt certificate.
+
+Everything you need is in this repo:
+
+- [`config/deploy.yml`](../config/deploy.yml) — the Kamal configuration, with
+  the four blocks you have to change marked `CHANGE ME`.
+- [`.kamal/secrets`](../.kamal/secrets) — the secrets Kamal passes to the
+  server, read from your environment.
+
+Fill those in, run `bin/kamal setup`, and you'll have Fizzy at
+`https://your-host` with data that survives the next deploy.
+
+
+## What you need
+
+- **A server.** Any Linux box with Docker installed that you can SSH into as
+  `root` (or as a user in the `docker` group, see `ssh.user` in the config).
+  Two cores and 2 GB of RAM are plenty to start. Ports 80 and 443 must be open.
+- **A hostname.** An `A`/`AAAA` DNS record pointing at the server, set up
+  before you deploy: Let's Encrypt validates over port 80 during the first boot.
+- **A container registry.** GitHub's `ghcr.io`, Docker Hub, or any registry
+  the server can pull from. You'll need an access token, not your password.
+- **A mail server.** Fizzy emails sign-in codes, so nobody — including you —
+  can log in without working SMTP. Any provider will do.
+- **Docker on your own machine**, to build the image Kamal pushes.
+
+
+## Fill in your details
+
+Edit `config/deploy.yml`:
+
+| Setting | What it is |
+| --- | --- |
+| `image` | Where your image lives, e.g. `ghcr.io/your-user/fizzy` |
+| `servers.web` | Your server's IP address or hostname |
+| `proxy.host` and `env.clear.APP_HOST` | Your hostname, e.g. `fizzy.example.com` — the same value in both places |
+| `registry.server` / `registry.username` | Your registry and its user |
+| `env.clear.SMTP_*` and `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS` | Your mail server and the address Fizzy sends from |
+| `builder.arch` | `amd64` for most cloud servers, `arm64` for Ampere/Graviton ones |
+
+Then put the secrets in your environment. `.kamal/secrets` reads them from
+there, so nothing sensitive ends up in git:
+
+```sh
+export KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD="…"    # registry access token
+export SECRET_KEY_BASE="$(bin/rails secret)"
+export SMTP_PASSWORD="…"
+```
+
+A git-ignored `.env` file with those lines works nicely too:
+`set -a && source .env && set +a`.
+
+Web push notifications need a VAPID key pair. Generate one once:
+
+```sh
+bin/rails runner 'key = WebPush.generate_key; puts "VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=#{key.public_key}"; puts "VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=#{key.private_key}"'
+```
+
+```sh
+export VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY="…"
+export VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY="…"
+```
+
+If you'd rather skip push notifications, comment those two lines out of both
+`config/deploy.yml` and `.kamal/secrets`.
+
+
+## Deploy
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+That installs Docker (if missing), boots the proxy, builds and pushes the
+image, creates the databases, and starts Fizzy. It takes a few minutes the
+first time.
+
+Now open `https://your-host`. The first person to sign up creates the account
+and becomes its owner; after that, signups are closed and new people join by
+invitation.
+
+Every deploy after that is:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal deploy
+```
+
+Migrations run automatically when the container boots.
+
+
+## Your data
+
+Everything that must outlive a deploy lives in the `fizzy_storage` Docker
+volume, mounted at `/rails/storage`:
+
+- `production.sqlite3` and its siblings for jobs, cache, and cable
+- `production/files` for uploads
+
+New containers mount the same volume, so deploys, rollbacks, and reboots keep
+your data. To back it up, snapshot the databases inside the container and copy
+them off the box:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal app exec --reuse "sqlite3 storage/production.sqlite3 \".backup 'storage/backup.sqlite3'\""
+ssh root@your-host "docker run --rm -v fizzy_storage:/storage -w /storage alpine tar -cz backup.sqlite3 production" > fizzy-backup.tar.gz
+```
+
+
+## Background jobs
+
+Solid Queue runs inside Puma (`SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA: true`), so jobs and
+recurring tasks run on the box without a second container competing for the
+same SQLite files. Give jobs more room with `JOB_CONCURRENCY`, and the web
+side with `WEB_CONCURRENCY`. If one container is no longer enough, the
+commented-out `jobs` role in `config/deploy.yml` moves them into their own
+container on the same box.
+
+Job dashboards live at `https://your-host/admin/jobs` for staff users.
+
+
+## Day-to-day
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal logs             # tail the app logs
+bin/kamal console          # rails console
+bin/kamal dbconsole        # sqlite console
+bin/kamal shell            # bash in the container
+bin/kamal app restart      # restart without deploying
+bin/kamal rollback [ver]   # boot the previous version
+bin/kamal proxy logs       # certificates, routing, health checks
+```
+
+
+## When something goes wrong
+
+- **No certificate.** Check that DNS resolves to the server and that ports 80
+  and 443 are open, then look at `bin/kamal proxy logs`.
+- **No sign-in email.** `bin/kamal logs` will show the SMTP error: Fizzy
+  raises on delivery failures rather than swallowing them.
+- **Health check fails on the first deploy.** The first boot creates the
+  databases and runs migrations; raise `deploy_timeout` in `config/deploy.yml`
+  if your server is slow.
diff --git a/test/deploy_config_test.rb b/test/deploy_config_test.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..60fc3c6
--- /dev/null
+++ b/test/deploy_config_test.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,59 @@
+require "test_helper"
+require "kamal"
+
+class DeployConfigTest < ActiveSupport::TestCase
+  test "deploys the app, its jobs, and its data to a single server" do
+    assert_equal [ "web" ], config.roles.map(&:name)
+    assert_equal 1, config.all_hosts.size
+
+    assert_not_equal "false", env("SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA").to_s, "jobs should run alongside the web app"
+    assert_equal "sqlite", env("DATABASE_ADAPTER")
+    assert_equal "local", env("ACTIVE_STORAGE_SERVICE")
+  end
+
+  test "serves SSL from the proxy for the configured host" do
+    proxy = config.role(:web).proxy
+
+    assert proxy.ssl?
+    assert_equal [ env("APP_HOST") ], proxy.hosts
+    assert_equal "/up", proxy.deploy_options[:"health-check-path"]
+  end
+
+  test "keeps the SQLite databases and the uploads in a volume that survives deploys" do
+    assert_includes config.volume_args, "fizzy_storage:/rails/storage"
+
+    production_sqlite_database_paths.each do |path|
+      assert path.start_with?("storage/"), "#{path} lives outside the volume and would be lost on the next deploy"
+    end
+  end
+
+  test "resolves every secret it references from .kamal/secrets" do
+    secrets = File.read(Rails.root.join(".kamal/secrets"))
+
+    referenced_secrets.each do |secret|
+      assert_match(/^#{secret}=/, secrets, "#{secret} is missing from .kamal/secrets")
+    end
+  end
+
+  private
+    def config
+      @config ||= Kamal::Configuration.create_from(config_file: Rails.root.join("config/deploy.yml"))
+    end
+
+    def raw_config
+      @raw_config ||= YAML.safe_load(ERB.new(File.read(Rails.root.join("config/deploy.yml"))).result, aliases: true)
+    end
+
+    def env(name)
+      config.role(:web).env(config.primary_host).clear[name]
+    end
+
+    def referenced_secrets
+      raw_config.dig("env", "secret").to_a + raw_config.dig("registry", "password").to_a
+    end
+
+    def production_sqlite_database_paths
+      databases = YAML.safe_load(File.read(Rails.root.join("config/database.sqlite.yml")), aliases: true)
+      databases["production"].values.map { |database| database["database"] }
+    end
+end
